<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georgi Dimitrov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georgi Dimitrov was a Bulgarian Communist leader who won fame in 1933 by defending himself at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> fire trial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, then headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> from 1935 to 1943. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, he became prime minister in 1946 and established a Stalinist regime, giving his name to the 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimitrov-constitution">Dimitrov Constitution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georgi-dimitrov.jpg" alt="Georgi Dimitrov" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TASS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgiy_Mihaylovich_Dimitrov_TASS_Portrait_Trim_Edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Georgi Dimitrov was born in Kovachevtsi, in what is now Pernik Province, the first of eight children of refugee parents from Ottoman Macedonia. His father was a rural craftsman who had been forced by industrialisation to become a factory worker. His mother, Parashkeva Doseva, was a Protestant Christian who wanted him to be a pastor, but he was expelled from Sunday school at age 12.</p><p>He trained as a compositor and became active in the labour movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>. By 1900 he was secretary of the Sofia branch of the printers' union. His brother Nikola joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, and Konstantin, a trade union leader, was killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a> in 1912.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Dimitrov joined the Bulgarian Social Democratic Workers' Party in 1902 and sided with the faction of Dimitar Blagoev, which in 1919 became the Bulgarian Communist Party. He was elected to parliament in 1913 and opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, for which he received prison sentences. In September 1923 he and Vasil Kolarov led a failed uprising against the government of Aleksandar Tsankov, which cost possibly five thousand lives among communist supporters, and he escaped into exile. A court sentenced him to death in absentia in 1926. From 1929 he headed the Central European section of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> fire on 27 February 1933 Dimitrov was arrested on 9 March. At the trial, which ran from September to December in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, he refused counsel and defended himself against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a> and the other Nazi accusers. On 23 December the court acquitted him and two other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, Vasil Tanev and Blagoy Popov, for insufficient evidence, and they were expelled to the USSR. Dimitrov became the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>, and the 7th World Congress of 1935 elected him General Secretary. In November 1937 Stalin told him to lure the German communist Willi Münzenberg to the USSR so that he could be arrested, and Dimitrov did not object.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dimitrov returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1946 after 22 years in exile and became prime minister after a referendum abolished the monarchy. In 1947 he signed the Bled accord with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a>, planning a federation of the Southern Slavs, but the plan fell apart over the future of the joint country and the Macedonian question, and it was abandoned after the Stalin and Tito split of 1948.</p><p>He died on 2 July 1949 at the Barvikha sanatorium near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Speculation that he had been poisoned has never been confirmed. His embalmed body lay in the Georgi Dimitrov Mausoleum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> until 1990, when it was buried in the central cemetery, and the mausoleum was demolished in 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georgi Dimitrov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The People's Republic was proclaimed on 15 September 1946, with <b>Georgi Dimitrov</b> becoming prime minister in November, and the Dimitrov Constitution followed in 1947.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-republic-of-bulgaria">People's Republic of Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kovachevtsi, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in Barvikha, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.55,22.82&amp;year=1925" aria-label="Kovachevtsi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Georgi Dimitrov"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4549 3921h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4549 3921h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.55,22.82&amp;year=1925">What was here in 1925? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
